WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.670
The DFT is

00:00:01.482 --> 00:00:02.425
just samples of

00:00:02.237 --> 00:00:03.368
the DTFT

00:00:03.894 --> 00:00:05.344
Eli Bendersky's new

00:00:05.156 --> 00:00:06.712
post tackles realistic

00:00:06.524 --> 00:00:07.632
signal processing

00:00:07.738 --> 00:00:08.717
where signals are

00:00:08.528 --> 00:00:09.872
both discrete and

00:00:09.684 --> 00:00:10.403
finite

00:00:10.942 --> 00:00:11.684
The trick

00:00:11.744 --> 00:00:12.557
treat your N

00:00:12.369 --> 00:00:13.559
samples as one

00:00:13.371 --> 00:00:14.220
period of a

00:00:14.032 --> 00:00:15.269
periodic signal

00:00:15.364 --> 00:00:16.484
so the discrete-time

00:00:16.296 --> 00:00:18.088
Fourier series reconstructs

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:18.866
them exactly

00:00:19.406 --> 00:00:20.950
Scale those coefficients

00:00:20.774 --> 00:00:21.434
and you get

00:00:21.246 --> 00:00:22.165
the DFT

00:00:22.354 --> 00:00:23.273
which turns out

00:00:23.085 --> 00:00:24.005
to be equally

00:00:23.816 --> 00:00:24.960
spaced samples of

00:00:24.772 --> 00:00:26.599
the continuous DTFT

00:00:26.812 --> 00:00:27.967
with Python code

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:28.698
to compute it

00:00:29.238 --> 00:00:30.252
Full story on

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:30.959
the dashboard
